WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.342 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can you tell whether a number divides exactly without doing
the whole division?

00:00:04.992 --> 00:00:07.853 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are using divisibility rules.

00:00:08.073 --> 00:00:15.217 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will test digits, combine two conditions when needed, and
explain what the result tells us about factors and
remainders.

00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:20.817 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to use divisibility rules and explain
why a test passes.

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:23.226 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:23.446 --> 00:00:25.911 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can choose the test for the divisor.

00:00:26.131 --> 00:00:29.374 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can use final digits or digit sums correctly.

00:00:29.594 --> 00:00:33.191 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can combine the tests for two and three to test six.

00:00:33.841 --> 00:00:36.824 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Divisible means division leaves no remainder.

00:00:37.044 --> 00:00:39.755 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For two, check the final digit is even.

00:00:39.975 --> 00:00:43.846 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For five, it must end in zero or five; for ten, zero.

00:00:44.066 --> 00:00:46.597 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For three or nine, test the digit sum.

00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:49.516 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For four, test the final two digits.

00:00:50.166 --> 00:00:53.574 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Is seven hundred and thirty-eight divisible by six?

00:00:54.224 --> 00:00:58.701 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A number is divisible by six when it is divisible by both
two and three.

00:00:58.921 --> 00:01:01.360 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its last digit, eight, is even.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.103 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The digit sum is seven plus three plus eight, which is
eighteen.

00:01:06.323 --> 00:01:08.827 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Eighteen is divisible by three.

00:01:09.047 --> 00:01:13.694 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both tests pass, so seven hundred and thirty-eight is
divisible by six.

00:01:14.344 --> 00:01:19.064 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Is one thousand two hundred and forty-five divisible by five
and by nine?

00:01:19.714 --> 00:01:23.203 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It ends in five, so the test for five passes.

00:01:23.853 --> 00:01:26.007 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its digit sum is twelve.

00:01:26.227 --> 00:01:30.747 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Twelve is not divisible by nine, so the number is not
divisible by nine.

00:01:30.967 --> 00:01:35.710 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Passing one divisibility test does not imply passing every
other test.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:40.490 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An even number is divisible by two, but not always by six.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:45.828 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Fourteen is even, yet its digit sum is five, which is not
divisible by three.

00:01:46.048 --> 00:01:49.155 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To test six, both conditions must pass.

00:01:49.805 --> 00:01:52.871 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Is three hundred and twelve divisible by four?

00:01:56.871 --> 00:01:59.329 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The last two digits form twelve.

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:04.533 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Twelve is divisible by four, so three hundred and twelve is
divisible by four.

00:02:04.753 --> 00:02:08.498 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check: four times seventy-eight is three hundred and twelve.

00:02:09.148 --> 00:02:12.808 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose the rule for the divisor and explain each condition.

00:02:13.028 --> 00:02:16.054 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Six needs both the two and three tests.

00:02:16.274 --> 00:02:22.701 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Divisibility rules are shortcuts for exact-division
decisions, and multiplication can confirm the result.

00:02:22.921 --> 00:02:26.329 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflect on which model helped you and explain why.

00:02:26.549 --> 00:02:29.660 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
